    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a helpful distinction. In the case where the interruption isn't due to physical damage, but rather a series of unpredictable and chaotic regulatory changes or local civil unrest that makes the premises practically inaccessible, how do courts typically interpret the \"unforeseeability\" element within the clause? Does it have to be something completely unprecedented, or is a general sense of chaos enough?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Singapore courts typically interpret the \"unforeseeability\" requirement pragmatically, asking whether the event was truly unforeseeable at the time of signing rather than demanding that it be completely unprecedented or invented for the first time. While unpredictable regulatory changes or civil unrest may not meet this high bar, they can still qualify if the specific gravity of the disruption was not contemplated by the parties when drafting the contract. Ultimately, courts will focus on whether the threat to access the premises was sufficiently remote and unavoidable at the time of the lease's inception, rather than requiring a novel event to justify non-performance.",
      "source": "target",
